<p>The syllable counter above uses the same regex heuristic as most implementations (it's ~85% accurate for English). The main inaccuracy is with compound words and unusual spellings, but it matches what MS Word's built-in Flesch-Kincaid reports closely enough for content editing.</p>
<p>If you want a C# version that doesn't depend on Office interop, the same logic translates directly:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public static (double GradeLevel, double ReadingEase) FleschKincaid(string text)
{
    var words = Regex.Split(text, @&quot;\s+&quot;).Where(w =&gt; w.Length &gt; 0).ToArray();
    var sentences = Regex.Matches(text, @&quot;[.!?]+&quot;).Count;
    if (sentences == 0) sentences = 1;

    int syllables = words.Sum(w =&gt; CountSyllables(w));

    double grade = 0.39 * ((double)words.Length / sentences)
                 + 11.8 * ((double)syllables / words.Length)
                 - 15.59;
    double ease = 206.835
                - 1.015 * ((double)words.Length / sentences)
                - 84.6 * ((double)syllables / words.Length);

    return (Math.Round(grade, 1), Math.Round(ease, 1));
}

private static int CountSyllables(string word)
{
    word = Regex.Replace(word.ToLower(), &quot;[^a-z]&quot;, &quot;&quot;);
    if (word.Length &lt;= 3) return 1;
    word = Regex.Replace(word, &quot;(?:[^laeiouy]es|ed|[^laeiouy]e)$&quot;, &quot;&quot;);
    word = Regex.Replace(word, &quot;^y&quot;, &quot;&quot;);
    var matches = Regex.Matches(word, &quot;[aeiouy]{1,2}&quot;);
    return Math.Max(matches.Count, 1);
}
</code></pre>
<p>This avoids the Office interop headaches entirely and runs in any ASP.NET environment without COM dependencies.</p>
<hr />
<p>If you need additional formulas beyond Flesch-Kincaid (Gunning Fog, SMOG, Coleman-Liau, etc.), the npm package <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/textlens" rel="nofollow noreferrer">textlens</a> computes all 8 standard readability metrics at once with zero dependencies — useful if your CMS has a Node.js backend or build step. <em>Disclosure: I'm the author.</em></p>

Q: Index 1 out of bounds for length 1 error while creating the array automatically from the CSV file A: <h2>Poor CSV handling, not a Hibernate problem</h2>
<p>Your issue is poor handling of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comma-separated_values" rel="nofollow noreferrer">CSV</a> data import. Your input <strong>file contains a blank line at the bottom</strong>. A terminating blank line is common, and your code should allow for that.</p>
<p>Your issue has nothing to do with <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hibernate_(framework)" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Hibernate</a> nor <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jakarta_Persistence" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Jakarta Persistence</a>.</p>
<p>Tip: Use any of the multiple good CSV parsing/generating frameworks available in the Java ecosystem. Do not roll-your-own CSV processing.</p>
<h2>Terminal line</h2>
<p>Your error tells us the problem:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>java.lang.ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException: Index 1 out of bounds for length 1</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The array has a size of one. Your last line has a single field.</p>
<p>Then your code asks for the second element, with <code>tempArr[1]</code> meaning &quot;get second element&quot; per zero-based index counting. You are trying to access the second element of the array, but there is no second element. Hence the index-out-of-bounds exception.</p>
<p>That line with a single-field is almost certainly just an empty line meant to terminate the file.</p>
<h2>Re-creation</h2>
<p>We can recreate your situation.</p>
<p>If we split on an empty string, we get an array of one element whose first element (index 0) is an empty string.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>String input = &quot;&quot;; // Empty line.
String[] output = input.split( &quot;,&quot; );
IO.println( &quot;Length (size) of array: &quot; + output.length );  // Size of array is 1, there is a single element.
IO.println( &quot;Count code points in element index 0: &quot; + output[ 0 ].codePoints().count() ); // But that element is an empty string.
IO.println( output[ 1 ] ); // Throws java.lang.ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException: Index 1 out of bounds for length 1.
IO.println( &quot;output = &quot; + Arrays.toString( output ) );  // Before Java 25, swap out `IO` for `System.out`. 
</code></pre>
<p>When run:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>Length (size) of array: 1
Count code points in element index 0: 0
Exception in thread &quot;main&quot; java.lang.ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException: Index 1 out of bounds for length 1
    at Ex23@1.0-SNAPSHOT/work.basil.example.csv.ExTrySplitOnEmptyText.main(ExTrySplitOnEmptyText.java:13)
</code></pre>
<h2>Solution</h2>
<p>Either you need to tell the publisher of your data to stop including an extra blank line at the end, or you need to deal with that blank line.</p>
<p>To deal with that blank line, detect it. Check the size of array returned by <code>split</code>. Here is a simplistic example:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>String[] tempArr = line.split(&quot;,&quot;);
if ( temper.length == 1 ) { … do last line stuff … }
else if ( temper.length == 7 ) { … do line processing stuff … }
else { … do error-handling stuff … }
</code></pre>
<h2>Solution in modern Java</h2>
<p>If you insist on managing the CSV yourself rather than use a CSV framework, do something like the following using modern Java facilities like NIO.2 and streams.</p>
<p>Specify a place for the file.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>Path path = Paths.get( System.getProperty( &quot;user.home&quot; ) , &quot;example.csv&quot; );
</code></pre>
<p>Write your sample data to file.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>String content = &quot;&quot;&quot;
        1,Reanna,Colepio,159031625,reanna_colepio123@gmail.com,London,Glasgow
        2,Rita,Das,987443767,ritadas@outlook.com,Edinburgh,Glasgow

        &quot;&quot;&quot;;
try
{
    Files.writeString( path , content ); // Writes UTF-8.
    IO.println( &quot;INFO String written to file: &quot; + path.toAbsolutePath() );
}
catch ( IOException e )
{
    System.err.println( &quot;ERROR Error writing to file: &quot; + e.getMessage() );
    e.printStackTrace();
}
</code></pre>
<p>Define a <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/395" rel="nofollow noreferrer">record</a> class to for each entity parsed from a line. Add a static factory method to process our array of fields.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>record Delivery( int id , String firstName , String LastName , String phone , String email , String pickup , String destination )
{
    static Delivery from ( final String[] fields )
    {
        return new Delivery(
                Integer.parseInt( fields[ 0 ] ) ,
                fields[ 1 ] ,
                fields[ 2 ] ,
                fields[ 3 ] ,
                fields[ 4 ] ,
                fields[ 5 ] ,
                fields[ 6 ]
        );
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Read the file as a stream of lines. For each line, pass to the static factory of <code>Delivery</code> record class. We get back a <code>Delivery</code> object, which we dump to console.</p>
<p>We use try-with-resources syntax to automatically close both the stream and its backing file.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>try (
        Stream &lt; String &gt; lineStream = Files.lines( path , StandardCharsets.UTF_8 )
)
{
    // Process the stream: print each line
    System.out.println( &quot;INFO Reading lines from file.&quot; );
    lineStream.forEach( line -&gt; {
        String[] fields = line.split( &quot;,&quot; );
        switch ( fields.length )
        {
            case 7 -&gt; IO.println( Delivery.from( fields ) );
            case 1 -&gt; IO.println( &quot;« EMPTY »&quot; );  // Last line. Ignore line by replacing println with pair of braces `{}`.
            default -&gt; IO.println( &quot;ERROR Found &quot; + fields.length + &quot; fields in line:  &quot; + line );
        }
    } );
}
catch ( IOException e )
{
    System.err.println( &quot;ERROR Error reading file: &quot; + e.getMessage() );
    e.printStackTrace();
}
</code></pre>
<p>Example usage.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>Path path = Paths.get( System.getProperty( &quot;user.home&quot; ) , &quot;example.csv&quot; );
ExCsvWithTerminalLine app = new ExCsvWithTerminalLine( path );
app.writeFile( path );

app.readFile( path );
</code></pre>
<p>When run.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>INFO String written to file: /Users/basil_dot_work/example.csv
INFO Reading lines from file.
Delivery[id=1, firstName=Reanna, LastName=Colepio, phone=159031625, email=reanna_colepio123@gmail.com, pickup=London, destination=Glasgow]
Delivery[id=2, firstName=Rita, LastName=Das, phone=987443767, email=ritadas@outlook.com, pickup=Edinburgh, destination=Glasgow]
« EMPTY »
</code></pre>
<p>Entire code for your copy-paste convenience.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>package work.basil.example.csv;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Path;
import java.nio.file.Paths;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class ExCsvWithTerminalLine
{
    private void writeFile ( final Path path )
    {
        String content = &quot;&quot;&quot;
                1,Reanna,Colepio,159031625,reanna_colepio123@gmail.com,London,Glasgow
                2,Rita,Das,987443767,ritadas@outlook.com,Edinburgh,Glasgow

                &quot;&quot;&quot;;
        try
        {
            Files.writeString( path , content ); // Writes UTF-8.
            IO.println( &quot;INFO String written to file: &quot; + path.toAbsolutePath() );
        }
        catch ( IOException e )
        {
            System.err.println( &quot;ERROR Error writing to file: &quot; + e.getMessage() );
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    private void readFile ( final Path path )
    {
        try (
                Stream &lt; String &gt; lineStream = Files.lines( path , StandardCharsets.UTF_8 )
        )
        {
            // Process the stream: print each line
            System.out.println( &quot;INFO Reading lines from file.&quot; );
            lineStream.forEach( line -&gt; {
                String[] fields = line.split( &quot;,&quot; );
                switch ( fields.length )
                {
                    case 7 -&gt; IO.println( Delivery.from( fields ) );
                    case 1 -&gt; IO.println( &quot;« EMPTY »&quot; );  // Last line. Ignore line by replacing println with pair of braces `{}`.
                    default -&gt; IO.println( &quot;ERROR Found &quot; + fields.length + &quot; fields in line:  &quot; + line );
                }
            } );
        }
        catch ( IOException e )
        {
            System.err.println( &quot;ERROR Error reading file: &quot; + e.getMessage() );
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

record Delivery( int id , String firstName , String LastName , String phone , String email , String pickup , String destination )
{
    static Delivery from ( final String[] fields )
    {
        return new Delivery(
                Integer.parseInt( fields[ 0 ] ) ,
                fields[ 1 ] ,
                fields[ 2 ] ,
                fields[ 3 ] ,
                fields[ 4 ] ,
                fields[ 5 ] ,
                fields[ 6 ]
        );
    }
}

    static void main ( )
    {
        Path path = Paths.get( System.getProperty( &quot;user.home&quot; ) , &quot;example.csv&quot; );
        ExCsvWithTerminalLine app = new ExCsvWithTerminalLine( );
        app.writeFile( path );

        app.readFile( path );
    }
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>In real work, I would put <a href="https://jspecify.dev/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">JSpecify</a> <code>@NonNull</code> annotations on those parameters. And I would perform null-checks with <code>Objects.requireNonNull</code>. I would do validation too, such as the <code>fields</code> argument having exactly 7 elements, and the strings being non-blank, on <code>Delivery</code> class.</p>
